          ---------------------------------
             Gavare's eXperimental Emulator
          ---------------------------------



Overview  --  What is GXemul?
-----------------------------

This is a fork of Anders Gavare's eXperimental Emulator GXEmul. It
builds on the amazing work of the original author, but with the
advantage of today's availability of Operating System installation
media and source code, real ROM dumps, documentation, fast host
machines, and coding assistance through AI.

GXemul was conceived as a framework for full-system computer
architecture emulation. It can model complete computer systems
closely enough to run unmodified guest operating systems as if they
were running on real physical hardware. It executes original firmware,
and faithfully emulates storage, accelerated 2D graphics, and
networking.

The emulator emulates (networks of) real machines. The machines may consist
of ARM, MIPS, Motorola 88K, PowerPC, and SuperH processors, and various
surrounding hardware components such as framebuffers, busses, interrupt
controllers, ethernet controllers, disk controllers, and serial port
controllers.

GXemul, including the dynamic translation system, is implemented
in C and should run on practically any modern host including Linux,
Windows, and Mac OS on Intel and Arm.

The documentation lists the machines and specific guest operating systems
that can be regarded as "working" in GXemul.


Possible uses of GXemul include:

 o)  running guest operating systems in a "sandboxed" environment

 o)  compiling your source code inside a guest operating system which you
     otherwise would not have access to (e.g. various exotic ports of
     NetBSD or OpenBSD), to make sure that your source code is portable to
     those platforms

 o)  educational purposes, e.g. to learn how to write code for MIPS

 o)  hobby operating system development; the emulator can be used as a
     complement to testing your code on real hardware

 o)  preservation of computer history, by simulating old/obsolete hardware

 o)  simulating (ethernet) networks of computers running various
     operating systems, to study their interaction with each other

 o)  debugging code in general


GXemul's limitations
--------------------

 o)  GXemul does not simulate micro-architectural things such as pipeline
     stalls, instruction latency effects, out-of-order execution etc.

 o)  CPUs and other hardware devices have been implemented in an ad-hoc and
     as-needed manner, usually only enough to fool guest operating systems
     into believing that the hardware devices exist and function
     well enough for the guest operating systems to use them.


Quick start
-----------

Packages, and the source, are attached to each release at
https://github.com/rpajarola/gxemul/releases, and are available from:

  Debian and Ubuntu (amd64, arm64): the apt repository at
  https://rpajarola.github.io/apt/, then  sudo apt install gxemul-rp

  macOS: the Homebrew tap at https://github.com/rpajarola/homebrew-tap,
  brew install rpajarola/tap/gxemul-rp, or the installer package from a
  release, which needs no Homebrew.

packaging/README.md describes what is installed and how the packages
are built.

To compile, type './configure' and then 'make'. This should work on most
Unix-like systems. If it does not, then please mail me a bug report.

If you are impatient, and want to try out running a guest operating system 
inside GXemul, read this:  doc/machine_decstation.html

If you want to use GXemul for experimenting with code of your own,
then I suggest you compile a Hello World program according to the tips
listed here:  doc/experiments.html#hello

Please read the rest of the documentation in the doc/ sub-directory for
more detailed information on how to use the emulator.


Feedback
--------

If you enjoy using GXemul, or feel like sending me comments or
feedback in general, please mail me at rp@servium.ch.

Many thanks to the original author Anders Gavare <gavare@gmail.com>.
